Let's consider an e-commerce website that sells books. We want to categorize the books into genres, such as Fiction, Non-Fiction, and Mystery. To achieve this, we can use HTML tags.

First, we'll create a basic structure for our book list using `<ul>` tag:
```
<ul id="book-list">
  <!-- Book items will be added here -->
</ul>
```
Next, we'll add individual book items inside the `<ul>` tag. We'll use `<li>` tag to represent each book item.
```
<ul id="book-list">
  <li>Book 1: Fiction</li>
  <li>Book 2: Non-Fiction</li>
  <li>Book 3: Mystery</li>
</ul>
```
Now, we can use CSS to style the books by genre. We'll target the `<li>` elements with specific class names using the `.genre` tag.
```
#book-list .Fiction {
  background-color: #f2f2f2;
}

#book-list .Non-Fiction {
  background-color: #ccc;
}

#book-list .Mystery {
  background-color: #666;
}
```
This is a simple example of using HTML tags to structure and style content, which can be extended to more complex scenarios in web development.
